[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 2"""
 date="2026-10-09T16:36:22Z"
 content="""
A way to fix this while keeping autoenable could be to make git-annex
refuse to push annexed files to remotes by default after autoenabling a
special remote. 

The UI for this could look something like:

	> git-annex sync
	[...]
	(autoenabling special remote "foo")
	[...]
	> git-annex copy --to origin
	git-annex: Error, sending files to special remotes is disabled
	because the recently autoenabled special remote "foo" has not been
	verified to be safe. Please examine the configuration of this
	special remote and make sure it is not accessing any resource that
	you would not expect it to access. If all looks well,
	run "git-annex verified-safe foo"

That relies on the configuration of special remotes being something that
a) the user can verify to be safe and b) won't change out from under them
in a subsequent `git pull` of the git-annex branch. Unfortunately, that is
not actually the case for all of them. Eg for a S3 bucket the bucket= field
in the remote config can be changed by a later git pull. It doesn't use 
git config like eg the directory special remote does. So this might also
need fixes to lots of special remotes.

The other problem with this fix is that it still lets the attacker
learn if I do have a given key in a location that they hypothesized.
`git-annex sync` will get it from the autoenabled special remote,
and update location tracking to say it's in my clone of the repository.
Then any `git push origin` will expose that information to the attacker.
"""]]
